```c
int i = 0;
```
```c
while( vlc_object_alive (p_demux) && i < 40 && Demux2( p_demux, false ) > 0 ) i++;
```
```c
for( int i = 0; i < PS_TK_COUNT; i++ )
```
Reason:
By declaring `i` within the scope of the loop (using `int i = 0;`), it ensures that `i` is reset to zero each time the loop is entered. This prevents the logical errors associated with the previous declaration and guarantees that the loop iterates correctly based on the intended logic, thus fixing the potential for infinite loops or incorrect behavior.


